{
  "slug": "hibiscus",
  "title": "OpenStack 2026.2 “Hibiscus” Strengthens Trusted Infrastructure for the AI Era",
  "description": "OpenStack 2026.2 'Hibiscus' Release Strengthens Trusted Infrastructure for the AI Era",
  "body": "<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=37p9C0n0LLw\" target=\"_new\"><img style=\"margin-top: 10px; margin-left: 25px; float: right;\" title=\"OpenStack Hibiscus Release\" src=\"/assets/releases/hibiscus-banner.png\" alt=\"OpenStack Hibiscus Release video Image\" width=\"350\" data-url=\"/assets/releases/hibiscus-banner.png\"></a></p><p>OpenStack 2026.2 (Hibiscus), the 34th release of the world&#x2019;s most widely deployed open source cloud infrastructure software, is now available. Hibiscus strengthens OpenStack as trusted infrastructure for increasingly demanding workloads, advancing confidential computing and security while expanding support for accelerated, high-performance infrastructure.</p>\n<p>Around 600 contributors collaborated during the six-month Hibiscus development cycle, a 21% increase from OpenStack 2026.1 Gazpacho. Together, they delivered 11,500 code changes, a 28% increase release over release. During the same cycle, the OpenDev Zuul continuous integration system ran approximately 1.6 million jobs to build, test and publish software across the OpenStack ecosystem.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://releases.openstack.org/hibiscus/index.html\">Release Notes &amp; Source Code</a> | <a class=\"releaseButton\" href=\"/assets/openstack-map/openstack-map-v20260401.pdf?view=inline\">OpenStack Project Map</a> | <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=37p9C0n0LLw\">OpenInfra Live Episode</a></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>How OpenStack 2026.2 Hibiscus Advances Trusted Infrastructure</strong></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>The Hibiscus release includes enhancements across OpenStack services that strengthen security and trust, expand support for AI and other compute-intensive workloads, and help operators use infrastructure more efficiently at scale; more cycle highlights </span><a href=\"https://releases.openstack.org/hibiscus/highlights.html\"><span>can be found here</span></a><span>.&#xa0;</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\" dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Trusted &amp; Secure Infrastructure</strong></p>\n<p>As organizations run increasingly sensitive workloads on shared and distributed infrastructure, Hibiscus expands the tools available to protect workloads, isolate tenants and establish trust in the underlying infrastructure.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nova expands confidential computing support</strong> with AMD SEV-SNP and Intel TDX, providing hardware-backed memory encryption, stronger workload isolation and attestation for sensitive workloads.</li>\n<li><strong>Designate strengthens DNS security</strong> with enhanced cross-tenant isolation, stronger authentication and support for TLSA/DANE, while adding new tooling to help operators prepare for post-quantum cryptography.</li>\n<li><strong>Cyborg strengthens accelerator security and tenant isolation</strong> with tighter access controls and hardware management protections designed for multi-tenant environments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Infrastructure for AI &amp; Compute-Intensive Workloads</strong></p>\n<p>AI, machine learning and HPC are increasing demand for specialized compute and high-performance storage. Hibiscus expands OpenStack&#x2019;s ability to expose, manage and efficiently use these resources as part of a programmable infrastructure layer.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Nova and Cyborg expand support for accelerated workloads</strong> by enabling OpenStack to manage mediated devices such as vGPUs, giving operators greater flexibility in how accelerator resources are provided and managed.</li>\n<li><strong>Manila expands high-performance storage options</strong> with support for Weka and Lustre parallel filesystems for data-intensive AI, machine learning and HPC workloads.</li>\n<li><strong>Ironic and Nova improve bare-metal resource tracking</strong>, helping operators more efficiently manage the specialized and heterogeneous compute increasingly used for AI, HPC and other demanding workloads.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>More Efficient Infrastructure at Scale</strong></p>\n<p>Hibiscus introduces improvements across compute, networking and image management designed to help operators get more from their existing infrastructure while simplifying large-scale operations.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Watcher improves infrastructure utilization</strong> by making workload consolidation more responsive to both real-time usage and reserved capacity, while expanding automation options for managing unused resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Neutron significantly reduces networking overhead at scale</strong>, including a more than 15x reduction in the memory footprint of HA router monitoring.</li>\n<li><strong>Glance makes image distribution and management more efficient</strong> with parallel image imports, preferred-store downloads and expanded cache management capabilities.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Security Backed by an Open Development Process</strong></p>\n<p>Trusted infrastructure is not only about individual security features. It is also about how the software is developed, tested and maintained.&#xa0;During the Hibiscus development cycle, OpenDev Zuul ran approximately <strong>1.6 million CI jobs</strong> to build, test and publish software across the OpenStack ecosystem. Over the past five years, Zuul has run more than <strong>14.2 million jobs</strong> supporting OpenStack development.</p>\n<p>Security researchers and OpenStack contributors have also worked together to identify and address vulnerabilities, and at the center of that work is the <strong>OpenStack Vulnerability Management Team (VMT)</strong>, which coordinates the responsible handling and disclosure of security vulnerabilities across OpenStack.&#xa0;OpenStack, like many other open source projects, has had an influx of contribution from security researchers: 42 OpenStack Security Advisories (OSSAs) issued so far this year along with 13 OpenStack Security Notes (OSSNs). As always, the OpenStack Vulnerability Management team ensures that security issues are handled punctually and discretely.</p>\n<p>The VMT thanks these 70+ contributors for assisting in making OpenStack more secure.</p>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Abhishek Kekane</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Adam Harwell</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Afonne-CID</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Alistair Coles</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Andrew Bogott</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Arnaud Morin</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Artem Goncharov</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Benedikt Trefzer</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Boris Bobrov</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Brian Haley</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Brian Rosmaita</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Carlos da Silva</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Carlos Eduardo</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Chen YuXiang</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Christian Schwede</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Clay Gerrard</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Clif Houck</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Cyril Roelandt</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Daniel Bengtsson</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Dan Smith</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">David Mohren</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Dmitry Tantsur</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Doug Goldstein</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Douglas Viroel</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Dr. Jens Harbott</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Eduardo Gonzalez Gutierrez</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Erichen</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Erin Shepherd</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Ghanshyam Maan</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Goutham Pacha Ravi</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Gregory Thiemonge</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Grzegorz Grasza</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Hao Wang</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Himanshu Roy</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Hyeongeun_Ji</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">James Denton</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Jay Faulkner</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Jeremy Stanley</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Jingwei.Zhang</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">John Fulton</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Julia Kreger</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Mark Nelson</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Matt Crees</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Matthew Thode</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Matthias Runge</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Maxence Bornecque</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Melanie Witt</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Myunghyun Lee</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Nevra G&#xfc;rses Yakar</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Omer Schwartz</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Pierre Riteau</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Radomir Dopieralski</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Riccardo Pittau</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Rodolfo Alonso</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Rohan Das</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Rolix</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Saad Zia</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Sage McTaggart</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Sami Yessou</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Sean Mooney</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Sergey r0binak Kanibor</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Slawek Kaplonski</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Steve Baker</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Sylvain Bauza</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Takashi Kajinami</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Tatiana Ovchinnikova</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Thomas Goirand</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Tim Burke</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Tim Shephard</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Tore Anderson</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Tuomo Tanskanen</li>\n<li class=\"col-lg-3 col-md-3 col-sm-3\">Yuliang Xiao</li>\n</ul>\n<p>&#xa0;</p>\n<p><strong style=\"font-size: 1.1em;\">A Flexible Upgrade Path for Operators</strong></p>\n<p>OpenStack Hibiscus is a non-SLURP release. The Skip Level Upgrade Release Process gives operators the option to upgrade once per year rather than every six months while maintaining OpenStack&#x2019;s predictable six-month release cadence.</p>\n<p>Operators running OpenStack 2026.1 Gazpacho, the previous SLURP release, can choose to skip Hibiscus and upgrade directly to OpenStack 2027.1 Indri, expected in March 2027.</p>",
